Hint files are written in place instead of to a temporary file renamed into
place. The rename is what makes "a hint exists" mean "the hint is complete":
a hint that fails its CRC is rebuilt by a scan that skips the tail handling,
since a file with a hint was sealed. Before #476, recovery created the hint
file before it cut a hint-less file's torn tail, so a crash left a partial
hint over an uncut tail and the next open refused the database: unfocused
runs caught it twice in about 45 minutes, and in 6 minutes once #476 cut
before writing but still created the hint file before the scan. Since the
hint file is created only after the cut, a hint torn in place sits over a
file already cut, and the rebuild reads it: a 15-minute run of seed 1
survived. One 20-minute run reported a worker that exited on its own (code
2) at 546 s; it did not recur and its log was not kept, so it is not
counted as a catch. The unit test below fails the hint write between its
frames and its trailer and sees the torn hint.
Expected: NOT caught: since #476 a torn hint lies over a cut file; the
Guarded-by test covers it.
Guarded-by: "DB recovery: a crash while open writes a hint leaves no hint behind it\, and the next open recovers"

diff --git a/bytecaskdb/db_rotation.cpp b/bytecaskdb/db_rotation.cpp
index e0fa44a..7de7e55 100644
--- a/bytecaskdb/db_rotation.cpp
+++ b/bytecaskdb/db_rotation.cpp
@@ -212,7 +212,7 @@ auto DB::stage_hint(const DataFile &file, OnDamage on_damage) -> HintStage {
 void DB::write_hint(HintStage stage, const std::filesystem::path &dir,
                     const std::string &stem) {
   const auto hint_path = dir / (stem + ".hint");
-  const auto tmp_path = dir / (stem + ".hint.tmp");
+  const auto tmp_path = hint_path;
   const auto bytes = [&](std::uint32_t off, std::uint32_t len) {
     return std::span<const std::byte>{stage.arena.data() + off, len};
   };
